Yeah, I took the example from LFB, which made that exact point, but since
you're probably also taking it from LFB you probably knew that anyway.

I guess what I meant is that any time {ko'a djica ko'e}, there is really a
{tu'a ko'e} that has to be involved. I don't say this because this is how
the word is defined now so much as because this is how I actually see the
meaning of "want." If you "want" a thing there is something that you want t=
o
happen (or be true, or whatever) that's involved with it, and I really
can't, in my mind, find an exception.

As for that way of defining tolcaudji, that's defined with a {nu}, which is
pretty much what I was talking about. That makes perfect logical sense,
whereas I don't honestly see {mi djica ko'a} for a concrete {ko'a} making a=
s
much sense as {mi djica tu'a ko'a} does.

As for {nelci} et al., I guess it depends on what you really want. I know I
wouldn't like it if {mi nelci ko'a} meant that {ko'a} had to be abstract,
but then I do like it that {mi djica ko'a} means that {ko'a} has to be
abstract.

mu'o mi'e latros.

